## 7. Self-Containment Strategy (requirement 6)

| Dependency that would normally be external | How this template avoids it |
|---|---|
| Stylesheet (`<link rel="stylesheet">`) | CSS text read from `report.css` at build time, injected into a `<style>` block in `<head>` |
| Charts (Chart.js/D3 via CDN) | No JS charting used; magnitude/status is conveyed via CSS badge colors and table sorting order — sufficient for a static compliance artifact and avoids any `<script src="https://...">` |
| Icon fonts (Font Awesome) | Unicode glyphs (`&#9888;`, `&#10003;`) instead of icon-font glyphs |
| Web fonts (Google Fonts `@import`) | System font stack only (`-apple-system, Segoe UI, Roboto, ...`) |
| SHAP images | Not linked as `<img src="./plots/xyz.png">` — read as bytes and inlined as `data:image/png;base64,...` via `image_to_data_uri()`. This is the one place a real external artifact (a PNG) exists; base64-inlining is the standard pattern for "single-file, offline-viewable, emailable" HTML reports |
| JS at all | None required for this spec; if interactivity (client-side filtering) is wanted later, it would need to be inlined in a `<script>` block, not fetched — noted as a possible v2 enhancement, out of scope here |

Net result: the rendered file opens correctly via `file://`, has no network
calls, and can be archived/emailed/attached to a compliance filing as one
`.html` artifact — matching the requirement and the report's likely
downstream use (auditor/regulator review, not a live dashboard).

---

## 8. Regulatory Mapping — Interface Contract (Coordination Flag → Track 08)

This track only defines **how the template consumes** a regulatory mapping;
the actual Article 10 sub-clause text/mapping is Track 08's deliverable
(`research/track_08_eu_ai_act.md`), not authored here. The contract the
template expects:

```python
RegulatoryTag = dict[str, str]  # {"article": "10(2)(f)", "note": "short clause summary"}
RegulatoryMap = dict[str, list[RegulatoryTag]]  # keyed by metric_name (Core Four)
```

Example of the *shape* only (not real legal content — placeholder for wiring):

```python
regulatory_map: RegulatoryMap = {
    "demographic_parity_difference": [
        {"article": "10(2)(f)", "note": "<Track 08 to supply real clause text>"},
    ],
    "disparate_impact_ratio": [
        {"article": "10(3)", "note": "<Track 08 to supply real clause text>"},
    ],
}
```

**Flag:** do not let this stub content ship in the real report — it needs
Track 08's verified Article 10 sub-clause mapping before `report/generator.py`
is implemented for real.

---

## 9. SHAP Embedding — Interface Contract (Coordination Flag → Track 15)

Template expects a `dict[str, Path]` keyed by `f"{metric_name}::{subgroup}"`
pointing to a PNG on disk, one image per **significant, sufficient-sample**
row (insufficient/non-significant rows never get a SHAP slot rendered — no
point explaining a disparity that either isn't real or isn't statistically
supported). `image_to_data_uri()` handles the base64 inlining; Track 15 only
needs to guarantee the output format (PNG) and the naming/keying convention
above, or supply an equivalent lookup this contract can be adapted to. If
Track 15 lands on SVG instead of PNG (e.g. `shap.image_plot` variants),
`image_to_data_uri`'s MIME type (`image/png`) needs a one-line change —
flagging so it isn't silently mismatched at integration time.

---

## 10. Testing Notes (Coordination Flag → Track 18)

Not implemented here (research-only track), but worth Track 18 knowing this
template exists so tests can assert against it:

- Fixture: a small `list[MetricResult]` covering all three status classes
  (significant, insufficient, ok) plus one `disparate_impact_ratio` row
  with `metric_value < 1.0` and one with `metric_value > 1.0`, to verify
  `deviation_from_fair()` ranks both correctly as "far from fair" (not
  just the low-ratio side).
- Assert rendered HTML contains no `http://`/`https://`/`<link rel="stylesheet"`
  substrings (self-containment regression guard).
- Assert every `insufficient_sample=True` row's `<td>` cells render `"—"`,
  never a numeric string, even if a future bug lets `metric_value` leak a
  float (NFR-003 defense-in-depth, mirrors the dataclass guard).

---

